# TinyTokenizer optimization task list

Goal: reduce binding, editing, query, and parsing costs while preserving public APIs,
tree ownership, matching semantics, trivia, and source roundtripping.

Status: proposed work. Source inspection identified the opportunities below; no
optimization or fresh performance validation is represented as complete here.
The ordering prioritizes contained changes before larger algorithmic changes.

## Evidence and completion rules

- [ ] Record the baseline commit, runtime, build configuration, hardware, benchmark
      command, input sizes, and workload counts for each experiment.
- [ ] Capture fresh Release baselines before modifying each affected subsystem.
      Treat existing BenchmarkDotNet.Artifacts reports as historical evidence only.
- [ ] Measure elapsed time, allocated bytes, and GC activity using matched workloads.
      Use repeated baseline/candidate comparisons; investigate noisy results before
      accepting a performance claim.
- [ ] Keep candidates separately reviewable and reversible. Preserve public APIs
      and established owning algorithms unless an explicitly justified change is needed.
- [ ] Run builds and tests through subagents, following the workspace instructions.
- [ ] For each item below, require focused correctness validation, a second review,
      and an independent audit before marking it complete. Audit expected intermediate
      behavior against the entire contract before reporting a defect.
- [ ] Store commands, results, limitations, and adoption decisions under
      docs/performance/, linking each report from this document.

## 1. Eliminate unnecessary allocation during syntax binding

Sources: TinyTokenizer/Ast/SyntaxBinder.cs;
TinyTokenizer/Ast/GreenContainer.cs; TinyTokenizer/Ast/GreenBlock.cs.

Observation: BindChildren creates storage before knowing whether children change;
GetOriginalChildren repeatedly copies slots; ProcessChildSequence materializes output
even without a match. Successful matches use LINQ to extract child ranges.

- [ ] Add baseline cases for initial binding, unchanged full/local rebinding,
      no-match schemas, empty definitions, and actual changed subtrees.
- [ ] Delay replacement-child storage until the first recursively changed child.
- [ ] Delay sequence-output storage until the first successful pattern match.
- [ ] Compare directly with original slots or carry explicit change information,
      avoiding arrays created solely to test reference equality.
- [ ] Reuse existing immutable storage where available without assuming all
      GreenContainer.Children implementations are allocation-free.
- [ ] Preserve GreenBlock's opener/inner-children/closer slot model; avoid repeated
      delimiter-inclusive array assembly and redundant reconstruction copies.
- [ ] Replace Skip/Take match extraction with a direct range copy. Transfer builder
      storage only where ownership and exact capacity requirements permit it.
- [ ] Preserve reference identity on unchanged trees, nested binding, definition
      priority, already-bound node handling, delimiter trivia, and error nodes.
- [ ] Run binder reconstruction, syntax tree, green-node, roundtrip, and affected
      query tests; compare binding shapes as well as serialized text.
- [ ] Measure allocation and time separately for changed and unchanged cases.
- [ ] Complete second review and independent audit; link the evidence and decision.

Completion: unchanged traversal avoids replacement arrays where possible; changed
binding preserves behavior; measured results justify adoption without claiming that
unchanged local rebinding measures the cost of editing.

## 2. Batch edits that share containers and ancestors

Sources: TinyTokenizer/Ast/SyntaxEditor.cs; TinyTokenizer/Ast/GreenTreeBuilder.cs;
TinyTokenizer/Ast/GreenList.cs; TinyTokenizer/Ast/GreenBlock.cs.

Observation: Commit applies edits individually, reconstructing shared ancestors and
their child storage and metadata repeatedly. E edits in a wide N-child container can
approach O(E * N) reconstruction work.

- [ ] Establish real-edit benchmarks varying edit count, container width, depth,
      replacement size, and distribution across containers independently.
- [ ] Include successful edits that change the green root, with and without syntax
      definitions, rather than only rebinding an unchanged tree.
- [ ] Document existing ordering for same-position, overlapping, and ancestor/child
      edits, including trivia transfer, undo, and atomic commit behavior.
- [ ] Design batching within the owning editing/builder modules, initially for
      compatible non-overlapping edits to one parent.
- [ ] Apply compatible child edits in one reconstruction per affected container.
- [ ] Extend reconstruction to shared ancestors once the local batching contract
      is validated; preserve structural sharing for untouched subtrees.
- [ ] Preserve existing handling for incompatible edits until an equivalent grouped
      implementation is demonstrated. Do not silently reorder them.
- [ ] Verify rebinding targets remain valid after insertions/removals and cover
      mixed root-level and nested edits, without narrowing required binding scope.
- [ ] Validate replacement/insertion/removal, same-position ordering, delimiter
      boundaries, trivia, undo/redo, and error behavior against the existing path.
- [ ] Measure reconstruction cost separately from parsing, selection, and rebinding,
      alongside end-to-end Commit cost.
- [ ] Complete second review and independent audit; link the evidence and decision.

Completion: compatible batches rebuild shared structure fewer times, preserve edit
semantics, and show measured benefits across edit-count/container-width scaling.

## 3. Bound streaming parser storage and reduce dispatch allocation

Sources: TinyTokenizer/SimpleTokenReader.cs; TinyTokenizer/TokenParser.cs.

Observation: SimpleTokenReader retains consumed tokens in its list until parsing
ends. ParseToken also uses an iterator for each dispatch.

- [ ] Measure large streaming inputs with bounded consumer retention; distinguish
      parser lookahead memory from output tokens and retained source text.
- [ ] Inventory lookahead requirements and any rewind assumptions across parser
      call sites before changing reader storage.
- [ ] Replace unbounded consumed-prefix retention with compaction or a ring buffer
      that preserves all outstanding lookahead and EOF behavior.
- [ ] Validate long inputs, large lookahead, nested blocks, unterminated constructs,
      early enumeration termination, and enumerator disposal.
- [ ] Benchmark reader throughput and peak retained memory in addition to total
      allocations; verify storage scales with lookahead demand rather than total input.
- [ ] Separately identify single-result versus multi-result ParseToken branches.
- [ ] Introduce a direct result path for common single-result dispatch where it
      removes iterator allocation without duplicating parsing control flow.
- [ ] Preserve lazy enumeration, token order, multi-result branches, errors, and
      source positions; compare token streams with the baseline implementation.
- [ ] Measure dispatch changes separately from reader-storage changes.
- [ ] Complete second review and independent audit; link the evidence and decision.

Completion: consumed-token retention is bounded by reader needs and any dispatch
optimization demonstrates a separate measured benefit with equivalent token streams.

## 4. Match supported queries before constructing red nodes

Sources: TinyTokenizer/Ast/TreeWalker.cs; TinyTokenizer/Ast/NodeQuery.cs;
TinyTokenizer/Ast/NodeQueryTypes.cs; TinyTokenizer/Ast/QueryCombinators.cs;
TinyTokenizer/Ast/QueryRegion.cs; TinyTokenizer/Ast/SyntaxNode.cs.

Observation: traversal and sibling matching construct red wrappers before knowing
whether nodes match. Sparse-result queries may allocate for many rejected nodes.

- [ ] Baseline sparse, dense, absent, and early-exit queries over wide/deep trees;
      include sequences, regions, repeated queries, and newline queries.
- [ ] Define which built-in queries can evaluate entirely against green nodes,
      and which predicates require red-node context or observable callback ordering.
- [ ] Add green traversal/matching within the owning query infrastructure, reusing
      existing matching semantics instead of reimplementing query control flow.
- [ ] Materialize red results and required parent chains only when needed.
- [ ] Retain the established path for unsupported/custom predicates and combinations.
- [ ] Preserve result order, parent/tree links, positions, sibling indices, equality,
      selection modes, region boundaries, and schema-dependent behavior.
- [ ] Check newline/trivia and zero-width region semantics explicitly; do not assume
      every query result is a directly corresponding green child.
- [ ] Compare result identities/locations and callback behavior against the current
      path, including First/Last/Nth/Skip/Take and mixed query compositions.
- [ ] Measure allocation savings and time for both sparse and dense matches;
      assess parent-chain materialization costs before adopting caching.
- [ ] Complete second review and independent audit; link the evidence and decision.

Completion: supported queries avoid unnecessary red wrappers with equivalent public
results and a safe, validated path for queries requiring red context.

## 5. Reduce temporary allocation during green-tree construction

Sources: TinyTokenizer/Ast/GreenLexer.cs; TinyTokenizer/Ast/GreenNodeCache.cs;
TinyTokenizer/Lexer.cs; TinyTokenizer/OperatorTrie.cs; TinyTokenizer/TokenizerOptions.cs.

Observation: GreenLexer materializes simple tokens, eagerly creates trivia builders,
and can replace freshly created nodes to attach trailing trivia. Each lexer instance
also constructs an operator trie.

- [ ] Baseline full parsing and small edit-fragment parsing with varying trivia,
      comment, operator, string, nesting, and source-size distributions.
- [ ] Delay trivia-builder allocation until the first trivia item is encountered.
- [ ] Where practical, construct leaves with final leading/trailing trivia once,
      preserving existing ownership rules and recursive block behavior.
- [ ] Evaluate existing common-token/trivia caches before adding new caches; avoid
      retaining arbitrary source strings globally.
- [ ] Determine whether operator lookup data can be safely prepared once per stable
      configuration and reused across parses; verify option mutability and concurrency.
- [ ] Benchmark the contained changes independently before larger reader changes.
- [ ] Evaluate bounded simple-token lookahead as a separate candidate to replace
      full-array materialization, preserving the existing green parser's control flow.
- [ ] Assess source lifetime, lookahead requirements, and error recovery before
      adopting the reader candidate; share compatible infrastructure with item 3
      only where the reader contracts actually align.
- [ ] Validate exact roundtripping for whitespace-only input, comments, strings,
      delimiters, malformed input, Unicode, and EOF trivia; run concurrency checks
      for any newly shared lookup data.
- [ ] Measure end-to-end parse time, allocated bytes, and peak retained memory,
      including small fragments where setup costs matter most.
- [ ] Complete second review and independent audit; link the evidence and decision.

Completion: adopt only candidates with demonstrated gains and unchanged source/trivia
semantics; document the decision for full-array versus bounded-lookahead parsing.

## 6. Prepare binding patterns and reduce failed match attempts

Sources: TinyTokenizer/Ast/SyntaxBinder.cs; TinyTokenizer/Ast/Schema.cs;
TinyTokenizer/Ast/NodeQuery.cs; TinyTokenizer/Ast/QueryCombinators.cs;
TinyTokenizer/Ast/SyntaxTree.cs.

Observation: every child position tries definitions in priority order, repeatedly
checks schema resolution, and passes an ImmutableArray through an IReadOnlyList
matching interface. The current binding benchmark contains only three definitions.

- [ ] Extend binding benchmarks across definition counts, alternatives per definition,
      match density, shared prefixes, and long failing sequences.
- [ ] Measure binder construction separately from reused-binder matching and actual
      SyntaxTree binding/rebinding, which creates binders at call sites.
- [ ] Prepare schema-specific matching data once at an appropriate owner/lifetime.
      Preserve resolution behavior when query objects are reused across schemas.
- [ ] Verify and measure boxing at the ImmutableArray-to-IReadOnlyList boundary;
      evaluate one reusable sequence adapter per child sequence or a suitable
      internal matching representation without changing the public query API.
- [ ] Derive conservative possible starting kind/text constraints for supported
      patterns and use them to avoid impossible definition attempts.
- [ ] Preserve global priority and alternative order within candidate filtering.
- [ ] Retain unrestricted candidates for optional prefixes, negation, lookahead,
      custom queries, and other patterns without a proven starting constraint.
- [ ] Validate identical winners and consumed ranges for ambiguous matches,
      equal priorities, schema references, and zero-consumption combinations.
- [ ] Measure preparation overhead and retained memory against matching savings,
      including small schemas and one-shot parses.
- [ ] Complete second review and independent audit; link the evidence and decision.

Completion: prepared matching preserves exact selection semantics and demonstrates
benefits at relevant schema sizes without unacceptable small-workload regressions.

## Final verification and reporting

- [ ] Run the complete relevant test suites through a subagent after adopted changes
      are combined, including end-to-end editing coverage where supported.
- [ ] Re-run representative combined workloads to detect interactions between binding,
      editing, parsing, and query changes.
- [ ] Publish an evidence table with baseline/candidate timing, allocation, workload
      counts, uncertainty, and any peak-memory measurements.
- [ ] Distinguish implemented/validated improvements, rejected experiments, and
      deferred work; do not mark deferred opportunities as implemented.
- [ ] Link all evidence reports and record remaining risks or unmeasured workloads.
